### Comprehensive Senior Answer
Accessibility standards specify exact luminance contrast ratios:
1. **Text Contrast (WCAG AA)**:
   - Normal body text (< 18pt / 24px): **4.5:1 minimum** against the background.
   - Large text (>= 18pt or >= 14pt bold): **3:1 minimum**.
   - Enhanced AAA standard: **7:1** for normal text, **4.5:1** for large text.
2. **Non-Text & Focus Indicators (WCAG 2.2)**:
   - Interactive borders, icons, and focus rings must meet **3:1 contrast** against adjacent colors.
3. **Focus Styling (`:focus-visible`)**:
   - Removing focus outlines with `outline: none` without providing an alternative is a critical accessibility violation.
   - The modern CSS standard is `:focus:not(:focus-visible) { outline: none; }` and `:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid #6366f1; outline-offset: 2px; }`. This shows focus rings only when navigating via keyboard Tab key, but hides them on mouse or touch clicks.

### Code Example
```css
/* Modern Accessible Focus System in CSS */
button, a, input, select, textarea {
  /* Remove default ring for mouse clicks only */
  &:focus:not(:focus-visible) {
    outline: none;
  }

  /* High-contrast focus indicator for keyboard Tab users */
  &:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid #4f46e5;
    outline-offset: 2px;
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 4px rgba(79, 70, 229, 0.2);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
}
```
_Applies :focus-visible with outline-offset for keyboard users while eliminating unwanted focus outlines on mouse clicks._

### 💡 Senior Engine & Architecture Insight
> WCAG 2.2 introduces Focus Appearance (Level AA) requiring the focus indicator to have an area of at least the perimeter of the focused element times 2 CSS pixels and a 3:1 contrast ratio against both focused and unfocused states.

### 🎯 Probable Interviewer Follow-ups
- **Q: Why is using `box-shadow` alone sometimes risky for focus indicators in Windows High Contrast Mode?**
  *A:* Windows High Contrast Mode strips `box-shadow` and background colors. Using a real `outline: 2px solid transparent` or standard `outline` ensures the focus ring remains visible in Forced Colors mode.
- **Q: Does placeholder text inside inputs count towards WCAG text contrast?**
  *A:* Yes, if placeholder text contains critical instructions, it must meet 4.5:1 contrast, though standard UX practice recommends using visible `<label>` and helper text instead.

### Comprehensive Senior Answer
When building composite components like custom Select dropdowns, Menus, or Data Grids, placing `tabindex="0"` on every single row or option forces keyboard users to press Tab 50 times to skip the widget. Accessibility guidelines require **Single Tab Stop** with Arrow key navigation inside:

1. **Roving Tabindex**:
   - Set `tabindex="0"` on the currently active child item, and `tabindex="-1"` on all other items.
   - When the user presses ArrowDown/ArrowUp, change the active item's tabindex to 0, update the previous item to -1, and call `.focus()` on the new DOM element.
2. **`aria-activedescendant`**:
   - Focus stays permanently on the parent container element (`<div role="listbox" tabindex="0">`).
   - As the user presses Arrow keys, update `aria-activedescendant="option-id-2"` on the parent.
   - Screen readers announce the active option without DOM focus ever leaving the container. This is standard for Autocomplete search inputs and ComboBoxes.

### Code Example
```typescript
// Custom ListBox with Roving Tabindex in Angular / React
@Component({
  selector: 'app-custom-menu',
  standalone: true,
  template: `
    <ul role="menu" (keydown)="handleKeyDown($event)">
      @for (item of items(); track item.id; let i = $index) {
        <li 
          role="menuitem"
          [attr.tabindex]="focusedIndex() === i ? 0 : -1"
          [class.focused]="focusedIndex() === i"
          (click)="selectItem(item)"
          #itemRef
        >
          {{ item.label }}
        </li>
      }
    </ul>
  `
})
export class CustomMenuComponent {
  items = signal([{ id: 1, label: 'Profile' }, { id: 2, label: 'Billing' }, { id: 3, label: 'Logout' }]);
  focusedIndex = signal(0);

  handleKeyDown(event: KeyboardEvent) {
    if (event.key === 'ArrowDown') {
      event.preventDefault();
      this.focusedIndex.update(i => (i + 1) % this.items().length);
    } else if (event.key === 'ArrowUp') {
      event.preventDefault();
      this.focusedIndex.update(i => (i - 1 + this.items().length) % this.items().length);
    }
  }
}
```
_Roving tabindex pattern ensuring only the active menu item has tabindex=0 while arrow keys update focus._

### 💡 Senior Engine & Architecture Insight
> `aria-activedescendant` is ideal for ComboBoxes (search input with dropdown) because focus must stay in the `<input>` element so the user can continue typing text while browsing suggestions with Arrow keys.

### 🎯 Probable Interviewer Follow-ups
- **Q: When should you prefer `aria-activedescendant` over Roving Tabindex?**
  *A:* When you have a search input with a dropdown popup (ComboBox), because focus must remain inside the text input for keyboard typing while Arrow keys select items in the popup list.
- **Q: What roles are required for custom dropdowns?**
  *A:* `role="combobox"`, `role="listbox"`, and `role="option"` with `aria-expanded` and `aria-haspopup="listbox"`.
